<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ndwandwe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ndwandwe Kingdom, Ndwandwe confederacy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ndwandwe were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> kingdom between the Pongola and Mfolozi rivers in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, the strongest power in the region in the 1810s under their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide</a>. They pressed the Swazi northwards and killed the Mthethwa ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a>, but were defeated by Shaka's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> around 1819. Their leaders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soshangane">Soshangane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a>, carried Ndwandwe traditions as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ndwandwe.jpg" alt="Ndwandwe" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SFC9394 · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Africa_topo_continent.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ndwandwe, also known as the Nxumalo, take their name from one of their early chiefs. Their chieftaincies were part of the Northern Nguni migration and settled during the eighteenth century between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pongola-river">Pongola River</a> and the Black Umfolozi River, where they subjugated and absorbed the surrounding groups. By the later eighteenth century, under their king Yaka, they had become dominant there; the Ngwane, later known as the Swazi, lived to their north, and the Mthethwa, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> and other groups to their south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide kaLanga</a>, king from about 1805, had several royal settlements, with his main capital on Magudu Mountain south of present-day Pongola. In 1817 or 1818 his armies destroyed the Mthethwa under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a> at Mbuzi Hill, south of Magudu, which left him the most powerful leader in the region. Shaka, a lieutenant of Dingiswayo, gathered the remnants of the Mthethwa and other neighbouring peoples against him, and in the first encounter of the Zulu Civil War, also called the Zulu-Ndwandwe War, Shaka's forces survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gqokli-hill">Battle of Gqokli Hill</a> in 1818.</p><p>In 1819 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide</a> launched a second expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>. Shaka let the Ndwandwe army into his territory and fought it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics until a shortage of supplies forced it to turn for home. On the retreat, as it crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mhlatuze-river">Mhlatuze River</a>, it was divided and decisively defeated in the Battle of Mhlatuze River. Zulu campaigns of retaliation destroyed every settlement and killed anyone they met, and over the next five years the Ndwandwe nation broke apart.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide</a>'s end differ. Oral traditions of Ndwandwe descendants in the Pongola Valley hold that Shaka's soldiers killed him soon after the battle on the mountain that now bears his name, and that he was buried in a forest held sacred by the AmaNdwandwe on the southern slopes of Magudu Mountain, where other Ndwandwe leaders also lie. Another version has him flee north with some followers to the upper Komati River and die there in 1825.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide</a>'s generals and sons led groups of the Ndwandwe north. One, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soshangane">Soshangane</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a> in present-day central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, and another, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a>, settled in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> as the waNgoni; others became chiefs of note in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>. Most of the Ndwandwe were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a>. Their descendants live today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, Mozambique, Eswatini and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, and speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> dialects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ndwandwe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pressed by the expanding <b>Ndwandwe Kingdom</b> of Zwide in the late 1810s, he led his people north into the hills of central Eswatini and settled in the Ezulwini valley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The region was dominated by two larger powers, the Mthethwa Paramountcy of Dingiswayo and the <b>Ndwandwe Kingdom</b> of Zwide, competing for land, cattle and control of trade with the Portuguese at Delagoa Bay.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…of King Senzangakhona, served as a warrior under Dingiswayo of the Mthethwa, who helped him become king of the Zulu; after Dingiswayo’s death around 1816 he led the whole Mthethwa alliance, defeated the <b>Ndwandwe</b> at the Mhlatuze River in 1820 and by 1822 ruled some 210,000 square kilometres.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Soshangane, a commander of Zwide's Ndwandwe Kingdom, fled north after the <b>Ndwandwe</b> defeat by the Zulu and by about 1824 had settled in southern Mozambique.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Ndwandwe</b> were settled around the Mkhuze and Pongola rivers in the north of present-day KwaZulu-Natal, with ties to the Dlamini of the Swazi Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/ndwandwe-kingdom">Ndwandwe Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
